// Q1 – Total Revenue, Average Order Value (AOV), Total Orders

db.getCollection('orders').aggregate(
  [
    {
      $match: {
        status: {
          $in: [
            'delivered',
            'shipped',
            'processing'
          ]
        }
      }
    },
    {
      $group: {
        _id: null,
        totalRevenue: {
          $sum: '$pricing.finalPayable'
        },
        totalOrders: { $sum: 1 },
        avgorderValue: {
          $avg: '$pricing.finalPayable'
        },
        maxOrder: {
          $max: '$pricing.finalPayable'
        },
        minOrder: {
          $min: '$pricing.finalPayable'
        }
      }
    }
  ],
  { maxTimeMS: 60000, allowDiskUse: true }
);

$match should almost always come first
Filtering early reduces the number of documents that later stages have to process → better performance.
_id: null
When you want one single summary for the whole collection (or the filtered set), use _id: null.
$sum: 1
This is the standard way to count documents in a $group stage.
$avg
MongoDB automatically calculates the average. You don’t need to do totalRevenue / totalOrders yourself.
Nested field access
"$pricing.finalPayable" works because in your order documents the pricing breakdown is stored like this:
----------------

Q2. Daily / Monthly Sales
Question - Calculate monthly sales revenue and number of orders.
(Also show how to do daily sales.)

db.getCollection('orders').aggregate(
  [
    { $match: { status: 'delivered' } },
    {
      $group: {
        _id: {
          year: { $year: '$createdAt' },
          month: { $month: '$createdAt' },
          day: { $dayOfMonth: '$createdAt' }
        },
        revenue: {
          $sum: '$pricing.finalPayable'
        },
        orders: { $sum: 1 }
      }
    },
    {
      $sort: {
        '_id.year': 1,
        '_id.month': 1,
        '_id.day': 1
      }
    }
  ],
  { maxTimeMS: 60000, allowDiskUse: true }
);


Stage,              What it does,                      Why it is needed

$match,   Filters only "delivered" orders  ,We usually don’t count cancelled/pending orders as real sales

$group,    Creates one document per Year-Month combination,  $year and $month extract parts from the createdAt date

$sum:"$pricing.finalPayable",       Adds the final amount paid by customer,            This is the real revenue

$sum: 1,   Counts how many orders are in that month,         Gives order volume

$sort,    Sorts results from oldest to newest,         Makes the output readable

----------------
Q3. Revenue by Category
Question - Find total revenue and quantity sold for each product category (clothes, watches, perfume, etc.).
Answer

db.getCollection('orders').aggregate(
  [
    { $unwind: { path: '$items' } },
    {
      $lookup: {
        from: 'products',
        localField: 'items.productId',
        foreignField: '_id',
        as: 'prod'
      }
    },
    { $unwind: { path: '$prod' } },
    {
      $group: {
        _id: '$prod.category',
        revenue: { $sum: '$items.itemTotal' },
        qtySold: { $sum: '$items.qty' }
      }
    },
    { $sort: { revenue: -1 } }
  ],
  { maxTimeMS: 60000, allowDiskUse: true }
);

-----------------
Q4. Best-Selling Products (by Quantity & Revenue)
Question
Show the top 10 best-selling products ranked by quantity sold. Also show the revenue generated by each product.


db.getCollection('orders').aggregate(
  [
    { $unwind: { path: '$items' } },
    {
      $group: {
        _id: '$items.productId',
        name: { $first: '$items.name' },
        totalQty: { $sum: '$items.qty' },
        revenue: { $sum: '$items.itemTotal' }
      }
    },
    { $sort: { totalQty: -1 } },
    { $limit: 5 }
  ],
  { maxTimeMS: 60000, allowDiskUse: true }
);